<BaseLayout title="Methodology" description="The evidentiary and editorial standards used by UR NOT MAD ENOUGH.">
  <div class="shell policy-page">
    <header><p class="eyebrow">Editorial standards</p><h1>Methodology</h1></header>
    <div class="prose">
      <p><strong>Our reporting is built to make the evidence visible and the limits of that evidence clear.</strong> We pursue consequential facts with aggressive curiosity and draw conclusions with restraint. These standards guide the work; they are not a claim of infallibility.</p>
      <h2>One standard, three products</h2>
      <p><strong>The Dossier</strong> develops a substantial investigation through records, direct reporting, competing explanations and follow-up. <strong>The Brief</strong> explains one consequential, substantially established finding in the space it needs. <strong>The Ledger</strong> tracks verified developments and unresolved outcomes after publication.</p>
      <blockquote><strong>A Brief is short because the story is simple, not because the verification is.</strong></blockquote>
      <p>A Brief and a Dossier pass the same evidence, fairness, response, current-status and publication-review gates. A promising Brief may become the first public record in a larger Dossier; the later reporting must independently verify every new claim.</p>
      <h2>What earns coverage</h2>
      <p>We currently focus on Yukon. A failed service, unfair process, costly choice or accepted practice can matter without becoming a scandal. We ask what happened, who is affected and what a reader needs to understand. Clear explanation and persistent follow-up can add value to a story others have covered. We do not publish weak claims to fill a quota.</p>
      <p>Non-partisan reporting can question political actors and criticize policy or social norms. We make the factual basis and our reasoning clear, apply the same standards across interests and distinguish our judgments from official findings.</p>
      <h2>Respect the reader's time</h2>
      <p>The finding and its human consequence belong near the beginning. We use plain language, place essential qualifications beside the claims they limit and make detailed sources available for readers who want to check them. We distinguish documented harm from an attributed experience or a possible risk. We never invent a person, scene or consequence to make the account more compelling.</p>
      <h2>Primary and secondary evidence</h2>
      <p>We prefer original records: judgments, audits, contracts, accounts, legislation, transcripts, regulatory filings, official correspondence and data. Secondary reporting can establish context and identify leads, but an important factual claim should be traced to the strongest available source. We link directly to records whenever access, privacy and safety permit.</p>
      <h2>Fact, inference and allegation</h2>
      <p>A fact is something the available evidence directly establishes. An inference is a conclusion drawn from established facts and should be identified as such. An allegation is a claim not independently established. Where the record is incomplete, we say what remains unresolved instead of filling the gap with certainty.</p>
      <h2>From findings to action</h2>
      <p>Every Dossier and Brief opens with a short findings digest and links to lawful, relevant ways readers can respond. These may include contacting elected representatives or the responsible public body, using an official petition process, attending a public meeting, making a submission, requesting records or contacting an oversight office. We verify those links and identify outside petitions or campaigns without endorsing them. We do not manufacture a call to action when no relevant process exists.</p>
      <h2>Anonymous sources</h2>
      <p>Anonymity is not granted merely for convenience. We consider whether a source faces a credible risk, whether the information has significant public value, and whether it can be independently corroborated. Readers should receive as much information as possible about why anonymity was necessary and how a source was positioned to know.</p>
      <h2>Direct reporting and requests for comment</h2>
      <p>Our reporting process includes seeking answers from people who can resolve material questions: those responsible, people affected, records custodians and independent specialists. Their accounts can reveal consequences or explanations absent from public documents. We check factual claims and distinguish an institution's assurances from demonstrated results.</p>
      <p>People and organizations facing material criticism should receive a fresh opportunity to respond before publication, with specific questions, supporting records and a reasonable deadline. We represent credible explanations fairly, including those that weaken our premise. An exception requires an editor's recorded reason and an accurate disclosure of the reporting limit.</p>
      <p>This direct-reporting standard was strengthened on September 11, 2026. The two Yukon investigations published on September 10 disclosed that fresh requests had not been sent. Later inquiries are postpublication reporting; they do not change that history. We describe a request as sent only after it has actually been sent.</p>
      <h2>Automation and human accountability</h2>
      <p>Software and AI-assisted tools may help discover sources, organize records, test claims, draft text and validate the website. Their output is never evidence. Material claims must be checked against cited records, consequential language must survive the same fairness review, and a named editor must approve every Dossier and Brief before publication.</p>
      <h2>Corrections and updates</h2>
      <p>We correct substantive factual errors promptly and append a note explaining what changed. Clarifications, new documents and later developments may be labelled as updates. Quietly changing the record is not an acceptable substitute for transparency.</p>
      <h2>Conflicts of interest</h2>
      <p>Writers and editors must disclose financial, political, personal or professional interests that could reasonably call their independence into question. A significant conflict may require recusal or publication of a disclosure.</p>
      <h2>Document preservation and source safety</h2>
      <p>Links disappear and public systems change. When lawful and appropriate, we retain working copies, record retrieval dates and provide archive links. Public transparency does not override legal restrictions, source safety, confidentiality or personal privacy. Sensitive working material is kept outside the public website repository.</p>
      <h2>Evidence labels</h2>
      <p><strong>Confirmed</strong> means directly established by strong primary evidence. <strong>Supported</strong> means strongly supported but involving interpretation or an incomplete record. <strong>Alleged</strong> identifies a claim not independently established. <strong>Unresolved</strong> marks an important unanswered question or evidentiary gap.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
